@{ ViewData["Title"] = "Maze Explorer"; }
<div class="mb-4"><h1 class="hero-title">Maze Explorer</h1><p class="hero-subtitle">Find your way from S to E. Use the arrow keys or movement buttons.</p></div>
<div class="apple-card mb-5" data-maze><div class="maze-grid" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Maze. Use arrow keys to move." data-maze-grid></div><p role="status" class="mt-3" data-maze-status></p><div class="maze-controls"><div class="maze-keypad" role="group" aria-label="Movement controls"><button type="button" class="btn-apple-secondary" data-move="up" aria-label="Move up">↑</button><button type="button" class="btn-apple-secondary" data-move="left" aria-label="Move left">←</button><button type="button" class="btn-apple-secondary" data-move="down" aria-label="Move down">↓</button><button type="button" class="btn-apple-secondary" data-move="right" aria-label="Move right">→</button></div><div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2"><button type="button" class="btn-apple-secondary" data-maze-reset>Restart</button><button type="button" class="btn-apple-secondary" data-maze-new>New maze</button></div></div><p class="form-text mt-3">S = start · E = exit · solid cells = walls · outlined circle = you</p></div>
<details class="lesson-details"><summary>How it works</summary><div class="lesson-content"><ol><li><strong>Store the map:</strong> a 15 × 25 grid uses row and column indexes, with an entrance opening on the left edge and an exit opening on the right edge; 1 means wall, 0 means path, S marks the start, and E marks the exit.</li><li><strong>Generate a solvable maze:</strong> randomized depth-first search carves passages between unvisited cells, backtracking at dead ends until every passage cell is connected.</li><li><strong>Check each move:</strong> arrow keys or buttons change one coordinate, then bounds and wall checks decide whether the player can enter the next cell; only valid moves increase the counter.</li><li><strong>Reach the exit:</strong> entering E ends movement and reports your move count; Restart replays the same layout, while New maze or reloading generates another layout.</li></ol></div></details>
<details class="lesson-details">
    <summary>C# code</summary>
    <div class="lesson-content"><p>The interactive simulation runs in JavaScript; this C# equivalent highlights the same two-dimensional array operations.</p>
        <div class="macos-code-box">
            <div class="macos-code-header"><div class="code-title">Maze — core 2D array logic</div><button type="button" class="btn-copy-code" data-code-target="MazeArrayCode">Copy code</button></div>
            <pre class="macos-code-body" id="MazeArrayCode"><code>// C# equivalent of the interactive maze's row-and-column movement.
bool TryMove(char[,] map, ref int row, ref int column,
    int rowChange, int columnChange)
{
    int nextRow = row + rowChange;
    int nextColumn = column + columnChange;
    if (nextRow &lt; 0 || nextRow &gt;= map.GetLength(0) ||
        nextColumn &lt; 0 || nextColumn &gt;= map.GetLength(1)) return false;
    <mark class="array-code-highlight">if (map[nextRow, nextColumn] == '1') return false;</mark>
    row = nextRow;
    column = nextColumn;
    return true;
}

<mark class="array-code-highlight">char[,] maze = {</mark>
    { '1', '1', '1', '1', '1' },
    { 'S', '0', '0', '0', '1' },
    { '1', '1', '1', '0', '1' },
    { '1', '0', '0', '0', 'E' },
    { '1', '1', '1', '1', '1' }
};
int row = 1, column = 0;
TryMove(maze, ref row, ref column, 0, 1); // Move right.
bool reachedExit = maze[row, column] == 'E';</code></pre>
